import{_ as a,c as t,o as n,ag as r}from"./chunks/framework.Vyheyqg9.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"React, Vue and Angular: Compare Mechanisms and Project Constraints","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"senior_growth/React/React_-_Vue_-_Angular_comparsion.md","filePath":"senior_growth/React/React_-_Vue_-_Angular_comparsion.md"}'),i={name:"senior_growth/React/React_-_Vue_-_Angular_comparsion.md"};function o(s,e,c,d,l,p){return n(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="react-vue-and-angular-compare-mechanisms-and-project-constraints" tabindex="-1">React, Vue and Angular: Compare Mechanisms and Project Constraints <a class="header-anchor" href="#react-vue-and-angular-compare-mechanisms-and-project-constraints" aria-label="Permalink to “React, Vue and Angular: Compare Mechanisms and Project Constraints”">​</a></h1><p>Baseline checked2026-10-21: React19, Vue3 and Angular21+ behavior. Angular22 is current in the checked documentation; zoneless became the default in Angular21. Existing applications can still explicitly opt into ZoneJS scheduling, so inspect their installed version/bootstrap configuration. This comparison supports the primary React learning path; it does not require implementing the course in three frameworks.</p><h2 id="philosophy-architecture-and-ecosystem" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">1 </span>Philosophy, architecture and ecosystem <a class="header-anchor" href="#philosophy-architecture-and-ecosystem" aria-label="Permalink to “1 Philosophy, architecture and ecosystem”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Concern</th><th>React</th><th>Vue</th><th>Angular</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>UI model</td><td>Components with JSX, props/state and composition</td><td>Components with templates or render functions and reactive state</td><td>Components/templates, services and framework conventions</td></tr><tr><td>State ownership</td><td>Built-in state/reducer hooks; Context transports values; external stores are optional</td><td>Built-in ref/reactive/computed; Pinia is a separately installed official store option</td><td>Signals and services; RxJS interop where appropriate; external stores are optional</td></tr><tr><td>Routing</td><td>Add a router/framework according to the application</td><td>Vue Router is a separate official library</td><td>Angular Router is a framework package</td></tr><tr><td>Shared dependencies</td><td>Context/custom hooks, explicit props or external DI patterns</td><td>provide/inject at component or application level</td><td>Hierarchical dependency injection with providers</td></tr><tr><td>Language/tooling</td><td>JavaScript or TypeScript; JSX/TSX</td><td>JavaScript or TypeScript; single-file components are common</td><td>TypeScript-oriented application tooling</td></tr><tr><td>Scaffolding</td><td>Supported React framework or an intentional build-tool setup such as Vite</td><td>create-vue/Vite and optional framework choices</td><td>Angular CLI and supported framework tooling</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Vue has dependency provision/injection; saying it has &quot;no DI&quot; hides a useful built-in mechanism. Pinia/Vue Router are not magically included in every Vue core installation. React has local state without installing a global-state library. Angular signals and explicit notifications matter even when a project also uses observables. Do not mistake the presence of an ecosystem option for a required architectural dependency.</p><h2 id="rendering-and-update-reasoning" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">2 </span>Rendering and update reasoning <a class="header-anchor" href="#rendering-and-update-reasoning" aria-label="Permalink to “2 Rendering and update reasoning”">​</a></h2><p>React schedules renders and reconciles elements; a component render is not automatically a browser DOM mutation. Vue tracks reactive dependencies and compiles templates to optimized update work. Angular uses its change-detection/runtime notification model; zoneless applications schedule updates through supported notifications such as template-read signals, bound listeners and explicit marking. ZoneJS is not required in Angular21+ default applications. Learn the actual state-to-render path before diagnosing an update that does not appear.</p><p>All three still depend on browser layout/paint, event handling, network/data ownership and accessible DOM output. &quot;Virtual DOM&quot; does not give a universal speed ranking. A framework choice cannot make an unbounded list, repeated synchronous parsing or a leaky subscription inexpensive. Inspect the application&#39;s rendering strategy, dependency versions, state granularity, batching and work done on the main thread.</p><h2 id="performance-bundles-and-scale" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">3 </span>Performance, bundles and scale <a class="header-anchor" href="#performance-bundles-and-scale" aria-label="Permalink to “3 Performance, bundles and scale”">​</a></h2><p>Compare the same feature under the same rendering/caching/deployment conditions. Record production JS/CSS transfer, hydration/server work, long tasks/interaction latency, memory and a realistic data/interaction trace. Include the actual router, state/form libraries, localization, UI components and assets rather than comparing one framework&#39;s core bundle with another complete application.</p><p>A small or enterprise application can be implemented with any of these choices. Team expertise, existing integrations, supported libraries, maintenance policy, accessibility and deployment constraints determine the trade-off. &quot;Angular is always slower/overkill&quot; and &quot;Vue is only for small apps&quot; are not measured design arguments. An opinionated framework can reduce integration decisions; a more assembled stack can fit specialized needs while increasing ownership of those decisions. Both still need clear module/data boundaries and tests.</p><h2 id="testing-and-migration" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">4 </span>Testing and migration <a class="header-anchor" href="#testing-and-migration" aria-label="Permalink to “4 Testing and migration”">​</a></h2><p>Use each stack&#39;s supported unit/component/browser tooling for the installed version. A DOM assertion, serialization snapshot and screenshot regression establish different evidence. Angular&#39;s testing defaults and CLI builders evolve; do not assume a historic Jasmine/Karma list describes every current project. Inspect generated configuration and official migration guidance before replacing a working setup.</p><p>Create React App is deprecated; it is not the default recommendation for a new React app. An existing CRA application needs a migration plan based on its actual build/routing/tests/deployment requirements. A client-only Vite setup and a server-rendering framework have different responsibilities; choose deliberately instead of treating scaffolding names as interchangeable.</p><h2 id="worked-project-decision" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">5 </span>Worked project decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#worked-project-decision" aria-label="Permalink to “5 Worked project decision”">​</a></h2><p>For this study project, keep React/TypeScript as the primary front end with explicit component contracts, owned server state and accessible native controls. If the team already operates a Vue3/Pinia or Angular signals/services application, transfer the underlying state ownership, cleanup, semantic HTML and request-race principles while adapting the framework mechanisms. Do not migrate a stable application solely because a generic comparison table assigns another framework a higher performance label.</p><p>Official references: <a href="https://react.dev/learn/state-a-components-memory" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">React state</a>, <a href="https://react.dev/learn/installation" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">React app setup</a>, <a href="https://react.dev/blog/2025/02/14/sunsetting-create-react-app" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CRA deprecation</a>, <a href="https://vuejs.org/guide/components/provide-inject" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Vue provide/inject</a>, <a href="https://vuejs.org/guide/scaling-up/state-management.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Vue store guidance</a>, <a href="https://vuejs.org/guide/scaling-up/tooling.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Vue tooling</a>, <a href="https://angular.dev/guide/zoneless" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Angular zoneless</a>, <a href="https://angular.dev/guide/di" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Angular DI</a>. Version-sensitive behavior checked2026-10-21.</p><h2 id="separate-practice" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">6 </span>Separate practice <a class="header-anchor" href="#separate-practice" aria-label="Permalink to “6 Separate practice”">​</a></h2><p>Apply the <a href="./../../practice/frontend/react/React_-_Vue_-_Angular_comparsion.html">focused requirements and failure cases</a>. All learner practice remains outstanding; the worked examples above are explanatory material.</p>',19)])])}const m=a(i,[["render",o]]);export{h as __pageData,m as default};
